Statements:
[
    "The harness memory management layer (MML) is the internal memory management component built into your harness.",
    "It handles memory storage, retrieval, context management, and memory updates as part of your engineering stack.",
    "Memory data stays under your control on your own system or harness.",
    "Data flow: The prompt arrives to the harness, MML fetches relevant memory and context from your local memory store, assembles the prompt including memory, sends it to the brain, receives the output, and updates the local memory store with new context and results.",
    "This enables continuity across model changes by swap brains without losing memory, and supports governance policies, privacy, and sovereignty.",
    "It requires engineering effort to design, implement, and maintain.",
    "Pros include full data ownership, easier enforcement of retention and privacy policies, resilience to external model outages, and portability across different models or brains.",
    "Cons include more upfront development work and the need to build and maintain the memory store and its integration.",
    "External memory storage in data flow refers to memory or state stored outside your system, typically in the model provider’s cloud or another external service, accessed via the model’s memory or tools API.",
    "It lives in an external memory store controlled by the brain or provider, and your data leaves your premises to the provider’s environment.",
    "Data flow: The prompt is sent to the model with memory fetched from the external store or memory updated remotely; the provider stores and updates memory as part of the model’s workflow.",
    "Implications: Faster deployment and simpler architecture, but data sovereignty, privacy, and compliance depend on the provider’s policies; there is risk if the provider changes or shuts down.",
    "Pros: Lower engineering burden, quick to implement, and it leverages provider capabilities (memory, retrieval, etc.).",
